Installing FERRUM
=================

    kubectl apply -k deploy

That is the default install, and it is the restricted one. There is no second
mode, no `--set enable-respond=true`, no values file whose defaults are the
convenient ones; what this root installs is the control plane with everything
this repository knows how to lock down already locked down, and every loosening
is a separate, deliberate act by an operator who typed it.

What that command installs
--------------------------

  * the seven CRDs from docs/crd — so an operator's first policy object is not
    rejected as an unknown type;
  * the `ferrum` namespace, labelled webhook-exempt, so a cold cluster cannot
    deadlock on the admission Pod that has not started yet;
  * the controller (ServiceAccount, RBAC, Deployment);
  * the webhook (ServiceAccount, RBAC, Service, Deployment).

It ends where deploy/admission/README ends its step 2: everything running,
nothing gating. Three things are still yours, and none of them can be a
manifest:

  1. the bundle signing seed, as Secret `ferrum-signing-key` in `ferrum`;
  2. the trust root the webhook pins, as Secret `ferrum-trust-root`, key
     `trustRoot` — the Ed25519 public key of that seed;
  3. the serving PKI, from `ferrumctl gen-webhook-pki` (see
     deploy/admission/README).

A kustomization that generated any of those would either put a key in git or
mint a new one on every apply. Both are worse than you creating them once.

Until the Secrets exist the Pods sit in ContainerCreating with the Secret they
are waiting for named on them, which is the failure said once, in the place an
operator looks. Until a ClusterSecurityPolicy exists and the controller has
compiled it, the webhook has no bundle Secret to mount and waits the same way.

Then, and only then, the object that starts gating the cluster:

    kubectl apply -f deploy/admission/validatingwebhookconfiguration.yaml

That file is rendered by `ferrumctl gen-webhook-pki`; the template beside it
carries `caBundle: REPLACE_WITH_PEM_CA_BUNDLE_BASE64` and is not appliable. It
is applied last, by hand, and it is not in any kustomization root — with
`failurePolicy: Fail` it refuses Pods the moment it exists, so it must never
arrive as a side effect of an install someone inherited.

The node agent is a separate root
---------------------------------

    kubectl apply -k deploy/agent

Not part of the default install, and this is a limitation rather than a design
preference. The DaemonSet mounts its BPF pin path as a hostPath of type
DirectoryOrCreate at /sys/fs/bpf/ferrum, which requires bpffs to be mounted at
/sys/fs/bpf on the node. A kind node has no bpffs there — /sys/fs/bpf is a
read-only sysfs directory — so kubelet cannot create the subdirectory and the
Pod never leaves ContainerCreating:

    MountVolume.SetUp failed for volume "bpf-pins":
    mkdir /sys/fs/bpf/ferrum: no such file or directory

Measured on kind v1.36.1 (kindest/node, aarch64), 2026-08-31. The repair is a
threat-model question and not a kustomize one — mounting /sys/fs/bpf whole
hands the agent the node's entire bpffs — so until it is answered this root
stays out of the install the gate proves, rather than the gate growing an
exception for one of its own resources. Apply it on a cluster whose nodes carry
bpffs; there is nothing else different about it.

`deploy/agent/optional-respond.yaml` is in no kustomization root either. It
replaces the DaemonSet with one carrying hostPID and CAP_KILL, and turning kill
from a logged decision into an action must be a `kubectl apply -f` somebody
typed, never a value somebody inherited.
`deploy_gate.rs::no_kustomization_root_installs_the_respond_variant_or_the_unrendered_webhook`
is what keeps both of those out.

Air-gapped: overlays/mirrored-registry
--------------------------------------

    kubectl apply -k overlays/mirrored-registry

The base pulls `ghcr.io/onixus/*`, which is the internet. That overlay rewrites
the image names into `registry.internal.example`, which is the only registry
`policies/examples/prod-restricted.yaml` allows — so the default install is one
the policy this repository ships would refuse if it were ever selected, and the
overlay is what makes the two agree. Substitute your own registry there. Do not
add a `newTag`: the tag is pinned once, in the manifests, and joined to the
release that can publish it by
`deploy_gate.rs::the_tag_the_manifests_pin_is_one_the_release_can_publish`.

It sits beside `deploy/` rather than under it because kustomize refuses a root
that contains a root it has already visited, and an overlay at
`deploy/overlays/*` naming `../..` is that cycle by construction.

Events to a SIEM: overlays/siem-syslog
--------------------------------------

    kubectl apply -k overlays/siem-syslog

The base install writes enforcement events to `events.jsonl` on each node and
nothing leaves the cluster. That overlay adds a second destination to the
agent's export fan-out — syslog over TCP or UDP, in RFC 5424 structured data,
ArcSight CEF or ECS JSON — and leaves the node-local file exactly where it was.
Second, never instead: the file is what an investigation falls back to when the
export counters say records were lost.

Not part of any install anybody inherits, for two reasons that are both in the
file. The address belongs to the site, and a default would be a name that does
not resolve on every node — which reads as every node Degraded and every event
counted as an export failure. And shipping records of what this cluster
enforces to a system outside it is a decision, in the same sense respond is.

What that overlay does not do is TLS. The transport is plain TCP or UDP, and
the records carry pod names, namespaces, process names and the names of the
policies in force. Put the collector on a network the cluster already trusts,
or point the agent at a local forwarder that terminates TLS for it; a TLS
client inside the DaemonSet would add a trust store and a renewal path to the
process the threat model calls the second target after the kubelet, and that
trade has not been made.

When the collector is not there: nothing waits and nothing is lost quietly.
Every undelivered event is counted in `export_write_failed_total` — the same
counter `status.json`, `/metrics` and `DEG_EXPORT_LOSSY` already read, not a
new one — and the node reports Degraded. What that counter does not say is
*which* destination failed; the agent's stderr says it, once per transition.

Why kustomize and not Helm
--------------------------

Four reasons, in the order they mattered.

The receiver. The wedge for this product is the closed contour — air gap, no
egress, an operator who already has `kubectl` and a mirrored registry and
nothing else. `kubectl apply -k` needs no second binary, no chart repository,
no OCI artefact to get across the gap, and no `helm` version to agree with the
cluster's. A chart is one more thing to mirror and one more supply chain to
verify for a product whose entire argument is that you should not have to trust
the vendor's channel.

The manifests are the artefact. Half of what this tree knows is written in the
comments of these files — why `defaultMode: 288` is decimal, why the bundle
mount is not `optional: true`, why tracefs is `type: Directory`. Helm would
turn each of them into a template, so the thing an operator reviews would stop
being the thing the API server receives, and every gate in this repository that
reads `deploy/**` would be reading a program instead of a manifest.

Values are a place for defaults to rot. A chart's defaults are a second copy of
the security posture, in a file nothing else reads, and the pressure on them is
always toward the convenient. Here there is one copy: the manifests. An
overlay can only add to them, in a file whose whole diff is the loosening.

Nothing was given up. What a chart would buy — one command, a rendered
namespace, image rewriting for a mirror — kustomize does in the four lines of
overlays/mirrored-registry. What a chart would additionally buy, release
tracking and rollback, this product does not want: the thing that must be
version-pinned and rolled back here is the signed PolicyBundle, and that is the
controller's job, not an installer's.

What holds all of this
----------------------

`install_gate.rs` applies this root to a real API server on a cluster that has
never seen FERRUM, and requires both Deployments to roll out. It is not a
reading of these files: last cycle two of the seven CRDs passed every
text-reading gate in this tree and were rejected outright by an API server.

`deploy_gate.rs::kustomize_roots` is the reading half, and it says so. It holds
that every manifest here is named by exactly one root or excused with a reason,
that the CRD root lists every CRD this repository ships, that the default
install is the restricted one, and that neither the respond variant nor the
unrendered webhook configuration is reachable from any root.

`ferrumctl lint-deploy` is the third, and it is about the manifests themselves —
a private key in the tree, a caBundle placeholder, hostPID without respond, a
projected token that disagrees with `--apiserver`.
